      <h1>Swahili Phrasebook for Zanzibar: Essential Words & Cultural Etiquette</h1>
      <p class="blog-excerpt">Learn essential Swahili phrases for Zanzibar travel. Greetings, numbers, bargaining, food, directions, and cultural etiquette. Includes pronunciation guide.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="blog-content">
      <section>
        <h2>Why Learn Swahili in Zanzibar?</h2>
        <p><strong>Kiswahili</strong> (Swahili) is Tanzania's national language and the lingua franca of East Africa. In Zanzibar, it's the primary language — English is widely spoken in tourism, but even a few Swahili words transform your experience:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Locals <strong>genuinely appreciate</strong> the effort (you'll get bigger smiles, better service)</li>
          <li>Bargaining in Swahili often gets <strong>better prices</strong></li>
          <li>It shows <strong>respect for the culture</strong> — Zanzibaris are proud of their language</li>
          <li>Emergency situations: basic words can be <strong>genuinely helpful</strong></li>
        </ul>
        
        <p><strong>Pronunciation tip:</strong> Swahili is phonetic — every letter is pronounced. Vowels: <strong>a=ah, e=eh, i=ee, o=oh, u=oo</strong>. Stress is usually on the <strong>penultimate (second-to-last) syllable</strong>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>🤝 Greetings — The Foundation of Zanzibar Culture</h2>
        <p><strong>Greetings are mandatory.</strong> Never skip them. Even a quick "Jambo" to a shopkeeper before asking a price changes everything.</p>
        
        <table class="rate-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Swahili</th><th>Pronunciation</th><th>Meaning</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Jambo</strong></td><td>JAHM-boh</td><td>Hello (generic)</td><td>Tourists, casual, safe default</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Habari</strong></td><td>hah-BAH-ree</td><td>How are you? / News?</td><td>Everyone, any time of day</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Habari za asubuhi</strong></td><td>hah-BAH-ree zah ah-soo-BOO-hee</td><td>Good morning</td><td>Before ~11 AM</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Habari za mchana</strong></td><td>hah-BAH-ree zah m-CHAH-nah</td><td>Good afternoon</td><td>11 AM – ~6 PM</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Habari za jioni</strong></td><td>hah-BAH-ree zah joh-EE-nee</td><td>Good evening</td><td>After ~6 PM</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Shikamoo</strong></td><td>shee-KAH-moh</td><td>Respectful greeting to elders</td><td>Anyone visibly older than you</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Marahaba</strong></td><td>mah-rah-HAH-bah</td><td>Response to "Shikamoo"</td><td>Elder replies to you</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Kwaheri</strong></td><td>kwah-HEH-ree</td><td>Goodbye (to one person)</td><td>Leaving</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Kwaherini</strong></td><td>kwah-heh-REE-nee</td><td>Goodbye (to group)</td><td>Leaving multiple people</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Tutaonana</strong></td><td>too-tah-oh-NAH-nah</td><td>See you later</td><td>Casual, friends</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Lala salama</strong></td><td>LAH-lah sah-LAH-mah</td><td>Good night / Sleep well</td><td>Bedtime</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        
        <div class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;background:#fff3e0;border-left:4px solid #ff9800;margin:1.5rem 0;">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0;">⚡ Greeting Protocol</h3>
          <ol style="margin:0;padding-left:1.25rem;">
            <li>Make eye contact, smile</li>
            <li>Say "Habari" or "Jambo"</li>
            <li>Wait for response ("Nzuri" = good, "Salama" = peaceful)</li>
            <li>Then ask your question</li>
          </ol>
          <p style="margin:1rem 0 0;">Skipping this is considered rude — like walking into someone's home without knocking.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

